From: ZBT WE826 debugging
Subject: [PATCH] rt2x00: clear external LNA cap too when NIC_CONF2 is blank

Follow-up to 998. On boards whose vendor EEPROM carries reference-design
defaults in NIC_CONF1, the external LNA claim (EXTERNAL_LNA_2G) is as bogus as
the external PA claim, and comes from the same word. Clearing only the PA bits
leaves the receive chain configured for a front-end that is not fitted:

  rt2800_config_channel()      RFCSR42 0xdb instead of 0x5b (chipver>1,eco>=2)
  rt2800_bbp_glrt_write(141)   0x1a instead of 0x10/0x15
  rt6352 calibration stage2    BBP 75/76/79/80/82 set to eLNA values
                               (0x68/0x4c/0x1c/0x0c/0xb6)

and the early-out in the RT6352 calibration path

  if (!rt2x00_has_cap_external_pa(dev) && !rt2x00_has_cap_external_lna_bg(dev))
          return;

is not taken while the LNA bit survives, so the whole external front-end block
still runs.

Measured on a Zbtlink ZBT-WE826 (MT7620A ver:2 eco:6) after 998 alone: the
client saw the AP at -38 dBm while the AP saw the client at -80 dBm -- a 40 dB
asymmetry on a reciprocal RF path, with receive throughput roughly half of what
the negotiated MCS should deliver.

Note this is weaker than 998 on principle: NIC_CONF2 has an EXTERNAL_PA field
but no corresponding LNA field, so this infers the LNA claim is untrustworthy
from the PA claim being untrustworthy in the same word. Justified empirically
for boards with an unprogrammed NIC_CONF2; would need a per-board opt-out
before going upstream.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ralink/rt2x00/rt2800lib.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ralink/rt2x00/rt2800lib.c
@@ -11429,6 +11429,8 @@ static int rt2800_init_eeprom(struct rt2
 			__clear_bit(CAPABILITY_EXTERNAL_PA_TX0,
 				    &rt2x00dev->cap_flags);
 			__clear_bit(CAPABILITY_EXTERNAL_PA_TX1,
+				    &rt2x00dev->cap_flags);
+			__clear_bit(CAPABILITY_EXTERNAL_LNA_BG,
 				    &rt2x00dev->cap_flags);
 		}
 	}
